// @emit-error `is` names a declared union member with no interned arm representation
// THE SHAPE THAT CAUGHT AN UNSOUND INTERNER MERGE (rep-fuzz seed 99, case_00243,
// `p2r ((i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32}) | f64`). A closure-arm value union whose
// RESULT is itself a union of a ref-list and a struct; the narrowing reads the ref-list
// arm out of the called result.
//
// It is a clean loud REJECT here, and that is the POINT of pinning it. Enabling the shape
// interner's element dedup (unifying the refinement's two vocabularies through
// `shapeFieldElemName`) makes this composition resolve — and it then compiles to a wrong
// answer: the `t1 is {f: boolean}[]` test takes the else branch and prints `OTHER` where
// `false` is correct. REJECT -> MISMATCH is the "silently worsen" transition
// `rep-fuzz-check.sh` exists to refuse, and MISMATCH is never baselineable.
//
// Nothing else saw it: 2,010 corpus files byte-identical, 2,159 suite cases green, 1,939
// wasm cases green, native fixpoint byte-exact. The rep fuzzer alone caught it. This file
// is the corpus-side pin so the next attempt at that merge fails here first.
//
// WHY THE MESSAGE MOVED (and why the reject did not). The floor used to answer here with
// ``is` names a type that is not a union variant`, which is false of this program: the
// tested `{f: boolean}[]` is spelled, verbatim, as a member of the very union `t1` has.
// The emitter's actual objection is that the ARM has no interned rep — the lambda
// `(q0) => ([{f: false}])` self-infers its single concrete return arm instead of the
// union box, so nothing in the program ever boxes a value into it. `emitIs` now separates
// the two failures; only the wording changed, the reject is the same one.
//
// THE THREE REPRESENTABLE NEIGHBOURS, measured, which is what keeps the floor narrow
// rather than a blanket refusal of the shape:
//   · a NAMED element type (`type S = {f: boolean}`, `S[] | {w: i32}`) — `refArmUnionRetName`
//     pins the lambda and the whole shape prints `false`;
//   · a NAMED producer function (`function mk(): {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32}`, `const v = mk`)
//     — the producer really boxes, and both the direct and the param-fed spelling run;
//   · the same union bound DIRECTLY (`const s: {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32} = [{f: false}]`)
//     — narrows and reads fine.
// Only the anonymous-element arm reached through a closure RESULT is the hole, and
// `refArmUnionRetName` declines exactly that cell on purpose (its `hasObjSibling` guard).
// Lifting that decline was tried and REFUTED: it does not fix this program, and it turns
// three construct-only neighbours that compile today into `array value does not match any
// array member of the union` rejects.
function makeIt(): ((i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32}) | f64 {
  return (q0) => ([{ f: false }])
}
function go() {
  const v: ((i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32}) | f64 = makeIt()
  const t0 = v
  if t0 is (i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32} {
    const t1 = t0(1)
    if t1 is {f: boolean}[] { print(t1[0].f) } else { print("OTHER") }
  } else {
    print("OTHER")
  }
}
go()
